---
title: On Re Branding Space in NYC
authors: [Matt Miller]
broadcast: 4
order: 2
start: "14:04"
---

CBGB opened at 315 Bowery in December 1973 and spent more than thirty years as the room where the Ramones, Patti Smith, Blondie, and Television played in the 1970s. The club kept its interior rough, covered in graffiti and stickers. Its lease ran out in August 2005 after a long dispute with the landlord, the Bowery Residents’ Committee, which sued for about $90,000 in back rent. A judge ruled that Hilly Kristal had never been properly billed for the increases. A December 2005 agreement let the club stay another year at $35,000 a month, up from $19,000. It closed on October 15, 2006. Patti Smith played about three and a half hours and ended with “Elegie,” reading the names of musicians who had died.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/matt-11.jpg" alt="Black-and-white photograph of a crowd under the CBGB awning at 315 Bowery, with the Palace Hotel sign beside it" /%}

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/matt-12.jpg" alt="Interior of CBGB, the stage and walls covered in graffiti and stickers" /%}

John Varvatos opened a menswear store in the same room in April 2008. The redesign kept pieces of the club: sections of graffiti and stickers under glass, framed photographs and posters, and playbills found behind a wall. Leather furniture, a chandelier, and clothing racks went in where the stage had been, and the store has sold T-shirts printed with the original CBGB logo. Varvatos told NBC, “We’re not recreating CBGB ... We’re not exploiting the name.” A protester at the opening shouted, “$40,000-a-month rents, $1,600 jackets and $800 pants are closing music spaces in New York.” The company filed for Chapter 11 in May 2020.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/matt-13.jpg" alt="Storefront of the John Varvatos store at 315 Bowery, a black awning over the former CBGB entrance" /%}

A few blocks away, 32 Second Avenue changed use in a different way. It opened in 1919 as a magistrate’s courthouse and jail, built three stories high after an earlier plan for fourteen stories and about a hundred detention cells. The court left in the 1940s, and the building became a Police Athletic League youth center. Anthology Film Archives bought it from the city in 1979. The architects Raimund Abraham and Kevin Bone converted it into theaters, a library, a film preservation department, and a gallery. It reopened in 1988 and sits inside the East Village/Lower East Side Historic District, designated in 2012.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/matt-14.jpg" alt="Anthology Film Archives at 32 Second Avenue, a three-story red brick former courthouse with arched windows, seen from the corner" /%}

In August 2017 the Landmarks Preservation Commission approved an expansion by Bone. It called for a library for roughly 30,000 film rolls then stored in the basement, a rooftop terrace, a cafe in a rear addition, and a new story clad in coated copper, topped with a metal mesh inscribed with the center’s name. The estimated cost was $6 million. Jonas Mekas said, “We are not doing this because of some whim that we want to build another floor or to have a cafe,” and explained that screenings do not cover operating costs.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/matt-15.jpg" alt="Rendering of the Anthology Film Archives expansion, a new copper-clad story on top of the brick courthouse" /%}

At 315 Bowery, pieces of the club sit inside a store that also sold its logo on T-shirts. At 32 Second Avenue, a building made to process and hold people now holds film, and its owners have gone looking for revenue in a cafe. Both kept something of what was there. Each chose what to keep, who it would serve, and who would pay.
